      "text": "First, let's find the prime factorizations of 11! and 12!.\n\n11! = 1 × 2 × 3 × ... × 10 × 11\n12! = 1 × 2 × 3 × ... × 11 × 12\n\nNotice that 12! contains the factor 12, which is also a factor of 11!.\nTherefore, we can subtract 11! from 12! to get the largest prime divisor.\n12! - 11! = (1 × 2 × 3 × ... × 11 × 12) - (1 × 2 × 3 × ... × 10 × 11)\n= (12!)(12 - 10)\n= (12!)(2)\n= 2 × 3 × 4 × 5 × ... × 10 × 11\n\nTherefore, the largest prime divisor of 11! + 12! is 2 × 3 × 4 × 5 × ... × 10 × 11, or 2 × 3 × 2 × 5 × 3 × 2 × 7 × 3 × 11.\nThe largest prime divisor is \\boxed{33}.",
