      "text": "First, we will determine the prime factors of 11! + 12!:\n\n- 11! = 1 x 2 x 3 x 4 x ... x 10 x 11\n- 12! = 1 x 2 x 3 x 4 x ... x 10 x 11 x 12\n\nThe term (12!) has two additional factors of 12 compared to (11!). So, 12! includes all the factors of 11! and also two extra factors of 12. Thus, 12! is divisible by all primes up to 11 that are not canceled out by 11!, which means all primes up to 19. Consequently, 12! has two additional dividend factors of 12.\n\n- Therefore, 12! has sixteen additional factors of 2, four additional factors of 3, and two additional factors of 5 from 12 including 2! x 3! x 5! = 3 x 4 x 6.\n\n- There are no other additional prime factors due to the fact that the only way you can add to 11 to make it divisible by one additional prime number is to take 11 * 1 to equal 11 * 2 which includes an additional 1. However, taking 11 * 1 is not factor of 12!\n\n- Therefore, the largest prime divisor is the largest remaining factor of 12!. Taking out all the previous factors of 2, 3, and 5. 10, 8, and 4 was 11! * 10 x 8 x 4 so then 12! would be 12! * 6 * 3 * 2 * 2 which distinguishes the remaining factor of 5. Therefore, 12! in its entirety must also be a factor of 12! + 11! = 1320!5.\n\n- The answer is 1320!5.",
